00:01:45.060 --> 00:01:52.960
Going all the way back to the Bible, plagues,
plagues and pharaoh, but also all through

00:01:52.960 --> 00:01:58.670
European history as infectious disease appeared
and disappeared.

00:01:58.670 --> 00:02:05.689
There were moments where it was viewed as
punishment from God for social sins, punishment

00:02:05.689 --> 00:02:08.920
of the individual for individual sins.

00:02:08.920 --> 00:02:16.010
And as we moved into a sort of more secular
moment that has transformed really in the

00:02:16.010 --> 00:02:25.720
19th and 20th centuries in2 sort of individual
punishment for some behavioral failing, whatever

00:02:25.720 --> 00:02:27.069
that might be.

00:02:27.069 --> 00:02:34.780
So sometimes it is, you know, drinking or
partying too much cholera.

00:02:34.780 --> 00:02:40.939
19TH century cholera was blamed on European
immigrants who were sort of a bit more lively

00:02:40.939 --> 00:02:44.330
than their counterparts in New York City.

00:02:44.330 --> 00:02:49.450
And then we're blamed for having cholera,
saying it was spread either by beer or occasionally

00:02:49.450 --> 00:02:50.450
by communion wine.

00:02:50.450 --> 00:02:52.870
That one was interesting.

00:02:52.870 --> 00:03:00.819
And then sort of as infectious disease has
spread this time, we've seen a lot of people

00:03:00.819 --> 00:03:07.799
talking about New Orleans and, you know, Mardi
Gras being the spot that spread it through

00:03:07.799 --> 00:03:13.000
New Orleans because those people needed to
party, which is not really totally fair to

00:03:13.000 --> 00:03:15.209
New Orleans.

00:03:15.209 --> 00:03:18.310
And it's it's still very present.

00:03:18.310 --> 00:03:24.560
I think in the ways that we talk about covered
and the ways that we have talked about disease

00:03:24.560 --> 00:03:29.370
spread now and really for the past several
thousand years.

00:03:29.370 --> 00:03:35.631
Unfortunately, the idea not just of the disease
as punishment for sin, but of prayer, maybe

00:03:35.631 --> 00:03:37.560
as a protector.

00:03:37.560 --> 00:03:38.700
How far back does that go?

00:03:38.700 --> 00:03:42.989
I mean, we recently saw this video of a woman
leaving a megachurch where she said she's

00:03:42.989 --> 00:03:44.489
covered in the blood of Jesus.

00:03:44.489 --> 00:03:49.930
So she's fine and so on and so forth, that
I'm guessing that's also not new.

00:03:49.930 --> 00:03:51.010
Nope.

00:03:51.010 --> 00:03:52.030
Also not new.

00:03:52.030 --> 00:03:54.810
Very much not new.

00:03:54.810 --> 00:03:56.830
It's.

00:03:56.830 --> 00:04:04.249
What's interesting is that the ways that that
worked kind of pre reformation are a little

00:04:04.249 --> 00:04:06.249
bit different.

00:04:06.249 --> 00:04:14.209
And it was more kind of a prayer covering
the whole community, but also a sort of acceptance

00:04:14.209 --> 00:04:18.489
that disease was part of life now.

00:04:18.489 --> 00:04:24.770
And in particular, in the United States, there
have been fights about national prayer to

00:04:24.770 --> 00:04:30.440
combat epidemics all the way back to the 18th
century.

00:04:30.440 --> 00:04:31.440
Right.

00:04:31.440 --> 00:04:34.561
In 1721, there was a large smallpox outbreak
in Boston.

00:04:34.561 --> 00:04:43.810
And there are fights about prayer and predestination
as they begin to introduce inoculation.

00:04:43.810 --> 00:04:49.440
And whether you should simply pray that you
survive, because if you're a good person,

00:04:49.440 --> 00:04:54.800
you'll survive or whether you should take
this inoculation, which at that moment was

00:04:54.800 --> 00:05:01.789
viewed as possibly, you know, violating predestination
by messing with the plan.

00:05:01.789 --> 00:05:06.440
And then in the eighteen thirties and eighteen
fifties, with cholera on the east coast of

00:05:06.440 --> 00:05:14.060
the United States, there are some impressively
intense fights about national prayer to prevent

00:05:14.060 --> 00:05:21.610
the epidemic that in some cases actually threatened
to unseat a variety of politicians and turn

00:05:21.610 --> 00:05:26.639
into political issues as well as religious
issues.

00:05:26.639 --> 00:05:30.639
So that one's old and very embedded in American
culture.

00:05:30.639 --> 00:05:37.419
So for me, this is a part of, at least right
now, seeing the current administration's handling

00:05:37.419 --> 00:05:42.150
of the virus, at least over the last month,
really as a culture war rather than a medical

00:05:42.150 --> 00:05:43.150
war.

00:05:43.150 --> 00:05:44.449
And I did a segment about this earlier this
week.

00:05:44.449 --> 00:05:48.090
My perspective is that they've essentially
failed on the medical war.

00:05:48.090 --> 00:05:54.430
So they're making it about culture and values,
which are much more pliable in terms of empirically

00:05:54.430 --> 00:05:56.169
determining what it is that's going on.

00:05:56.169 --> 00:06:02.790
And so masks have become a signal of your
perspective as to what's going on.

00:06:02.790 --> 00:06:10.120
Open versus closed has become an issue of
government tyranny and so on and so forth.

00:06:10.120 --> 00:06:14.669
Is this the way this often goes where it becomes
a culture war?

00:06:14.669 --> 00:06:17.500
Mm hmm.

00:06:17.500 --> 00:06:24.160
You know, honestly, having studied a lot of
disease history, I would say that this kind

00:06:24.160 --> 00:06:30.289
of polarization of responding to disease is
fairly unusual.

00:06:30.289 --> 00:06:33.220
The fights.

00:06:33.220 --> 00:06:38.210
So the fights around infectious disease do
often fall on cultural lines.

00:06:38.210 --> 00:06:39.289
Right.

00:06:39.289 --> 00:06:45.570
So there's there's an author I love called
Deserto who talks about the ways that in that

00:06:45.570 --> 00:06:52.750
in certain moments, the things that underlie
society, the tensions that are already there,

00:06:52.750 --> 00:06:55.860
kind of break apart and come to light.

00:06:55.860 --> 00:07:01.800
He talks about these tensions that exist that
we all live inside, sort of rising up from

00:07:01.800 --> 00:07:02.800
under the streets.

00:07:02.800 --> 00:07:08.229
It's a beautiful passage, but I think.

00:07:08.229 --> 00:07:15.490
The commonality among infectious disease outbreaks
is that they intensify and make very visible

00:07:15.490 --> 00:07:22.900
social decision, social divisions, social
tensions and existing problems.

00:07:22.900 --> 00:07:29.659
But rarely do you see this kind of.

00:07:29.659 --> 00:07:35.710
Pretending that a disease just doesn't exist
in communities where it does.

00:07:35.710 --> 00:07:42.419
It's I obviously am studying this as I'm working
on it because it's both fascinating and a

00:07:42.419 --> 00:07:43.910
little disturbing, too.

00:07:43.910 --> 00:07:45.280
To what do you attribute it?

00:07:45.280 --> 00:07:46.720
I mean, why why?

00:07:46.720 --> 00:07:49.730
It seems too simplistic to say.

00:07:49.730 --> 00:07:51.240
Well, it's because of Trump.

00:07:51.240 --> 00:07:57.580
I mean, he seems to be the one who is at the
top of the federal government right now.

00:07:57.580 --> 00:08:03.139
But there's some element of society allowing
that to take place as well.

00:08:03.139 --> 00:08:04.139
Right.

00:08:04.139 --> 00:08:05.139
Absolutely.

00:08:05.139 --> 00:08:14.600
I think that America for a long time has been
deeply divided along urban and rural lines.

00:08:14.600 --> 00:08:20.110
And that has very much played out and covered
where the early outbreaks were very much in

00:08:20.110 --> 00:08:23.050
urban areas where people travel more.

00:08:23.050 --> 00:08:26.830
And it's only now that they're really moving
into rural areas.

00:08:26.830 --> 00:08:35.110
So for a long time, the pattern of kov it
in the country actually exemplified that division.

00:08:35.110 --> 00:08:43.270
I think that America has for a long time had
some intense class division among types of

00:08:43.270 --> 00:08:44.690
workers.

00:08:44.690 --> 00:08:52.070
And that is also very much playing out here
where a lot of the a lot of workers simply

00:08:52.070 --> 00:08:53.279
can't stay home.

00:08:53.279 --> 00:08:59.540
And so for those who can, responding to the
disease is a different type of ask.

00:08:59.540 --> 00:09:00.540
Right.

00:09:00.540 --> 00:09:07.510
Asking me to work from home on history, historical
stuff or policy is a very different ask than

00:09:07.510 --> 00:09:12.850
asking that of someone who works in a factory
where working from home isn't an option.

00:09:12.850 --> 00:09:16.440
So some of it, I think is also about.

00:09:16.440 --> 00:09:23.420
The ways people have to justify their own
need to, you know, eat and continue working

00:09:23.420 --> 00:09:27.089
and feed their families.

00:09:27.089 --> 00:09:32.890
And then some of it is the intense polarization
that we've experienced over really the past

00:09:32.890 --> 00:09:34.790
10 years.

00:09:34.790 --> 00:09:41.250
I think that divisions and what news we watch,
what radio shows we listen to, what newspapers

00:09:41.250 --> 00:09:49.010
we read have really made it possible for there
to be multiple worlds based on the same information

00:09:49.010 --> 00:09:56.810
in a way that just hasn't historically always
been true when it comes to talking now about

00:09:56.810 --> 00:10:00.340
stigma of of disease itself.

00:10:00.340 --> 00:10:04.200
There's certain diseases that have more social
stigma attached to them.

00:10:04.200 --> 00:10:09.990
So certainly sexually transmitted infections
and diseases would be one category within

00:10:09.990 --> 00:10:11.100
cancer.

00:10:11.100 --> 00:10:17.970
Lung cancer sometimes generates less sympathy
because it can be connected to the bad act

00:10:17.970 --> 00:10:18.970
of smoking.

00:10:18.970 --> 00:10:20.480
Cigarets, as an example.

00:10:20.480 --> 00:10:27.389
Where does the where do these types of pandemics
that like Corona virus kind of fit into this

00:10:27.389 --> 00:10:30.700
stigma analysis?

00:10:30.700 --> 00:10:36.440
So I wish I wish dearly that I could tell
you that pandemic disease was an exemption

00:10:36.440 --> 00:10:37.839
from stigma.

00:10:37.839 --> 00:10:41.839
Unfortunately, I. I don't get to say that.

00:10:41.839 --> 00:10:43.760
So it's.

00:10:43.760 --> 00:10:51.529
Lower on the scale, then things like LCD or
lung cancer.

00:10:51.529 --> 00:10:53.630
Higher on the scale than zero.

00:10:53.630 --> 00:10:54.630
Right.

00:10:54.630 --> 00:11:01.740
So you're starting to see problems where people
have recovered from Cauvin and are having

00:11:01.740 --> 00:11:07.040
difficulty still getting ambulances to come
to their house or their neighbors don't want

00:11:07.040 --> 00:11:11.649
them to leave the house or want them to move
out of the neighborhoods, businesses where

00:11:11.649 --> 00:11:18.300
the owner has recovered, where no one will
go inside because they're afraid of the virus.

00:11:18.300 --> 00:11:21.639
And I think that that is something we will
probably see expanding.

00:11:21.639 --> 00:11:28.550
There has also, unfortunately, been a lot
of racialization of this virus that has cause

00:11:28.550 --> 00:11:34.209
serious problems in a lot of Chinese American
communities, but also, unfortunately, other

00:11:34.209 --> 00:11:36.889
